    - target: {fileID: 4459195616415204416, guid: aacf23fd940d08d419d1dcbc7feb3a70,
        type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: 'When looking at how light actually interacts with surfaces, there is
        a difference between metals and non-metals.





        For metals, the
        light can either be reflected or absorbed. How much depends on the incoming
        light angle and material and is described by the fresnel effect.





        If
        the surface is rough, the light is reflected in different ways. In addition,
        the geometry can mask itself which influences how light is reflected. These
        Microfacets aren''t modelled but instead described statistically with a roughness
        term. Instead of calculating it perfectly, different approximation functions
        exist.'
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 4539665407042477363, guid: aacf23fd940d08d419d1dcbc7feb3a70,
        type: 3}
      propertyPath: title
      value: BRDF textures
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 4539665407042477363, guid: aacf23fd940d08d419d1dcbc7feb3a70,
        type: 3}
      propertyPath: description
      value: 'To display material values that exist in the real world, the required
        data can be stored in texture maps.



        Besides the Albedo and Normal
        map, a popular way of storing values are ARM (Ambiend occlusion, Roughness,
        Metallic) maps:



        - Ambiend occlusion: Due to the surface geometry
        of the material, light enters certain parts less. This self shadowing is
        applied to the indirect ambient light.



        - Rougness: Since surfaces
        aren''t perfectly smooth, a statistical value is stored and used in the light
        behavior evaluation.



        - Metallic: While materials can only be metallic
        or non-metallic, pixels can have a areas of both metals and non-metals. Therefore,
        the value lies in the range of 0...1.'
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
